---
// Diagram: how a Project, standing instructions, and memory fit together.
// Three persistent-context mechanisms all feed a single chat, but they differ
// in who writes them: you write instructions on purpose, Claude saves memory
// on its own, and a Project carries whatever files and rules you put in it.
// Static SVG, token-driven colours; each mechanism is named and annotated so
// meaning never rides on colour alone.
---

<figure class="diagram">
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 460 230"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="mech-title mech-desc"
    class="diagram__svg"
  >
    <title id="mech-title">Three ways persistent context reaches a chat</title>
    <desc id="mech-desc">
      Three mechanisms sit in a row and all feed into one chat below them. A
      Project carries files and rules you put in its workspace. Standing
      instructions are rules you write on purpose. Memory is context Claude
      saves on its own. Each one feeds the same chat, but they differ in who
      writes them.
    </desc>

    <defs>
      <marker id="mech-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5"
        markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto">
        <path class="m-arrowhead" d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" />
      </marker>
    </defs>

    {/* Three mechanism cards */}
    <g>
      <rect class="m-card" x="16" y="20" width="132" height="60" rx="8" />
      <text class="m-name" x="82" y="44">Project</text>
      <text class="m-who" x="82" y="64">files + rules you add</text>
    </g>
    <g>
      <rect class="m-card" x="164" y="20" width="132" height="60" rx="8" />
      <text class="m-name" x="230" y="44">Instructions</text>
      <text class="m-who" x="230" y="64">rules you write</text>
    </g>
    <g>
      <rect class="m-card m-card--auto" x="312" y="20" width="132" height="60" rx="8" />
      <text class="m-name" x="378" y="44">Memory</text>
      <text class="m-who" x="378" y="64">Claude saves</text>
    </g>

    {/* feed lines into the chat */}
    <line class="m-feed" x1="82" y1="80" x2="190" y2="150" marker-end="url(#mech-arrow)" />
    <line class="m-feed" x1="230" y1="80" x2="230" y2="150" marker-end="url(#mech-arrow)" />
    <line class="m-feed" x1="378" y1="80" x2="270" y2="150" marker-end="url(#mech-arrow)" />

    {/* the chat they all feed */}
    <rect class="m-chat" x="150" y="156" width="160" height="50" rx="8" />
    <text class="m-chatname" x="230" y="186">this chat</text>
  </svg>
  <figcaption class="diagram__cap">
    All three carry context into a chat - the difference is who writes them: you
    write instructions, Claude saves memory, a Project holds what you add.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .diagram {
    margin: var(--space-6) 0;
  }
  .diagram__svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 32rem;
    height: auto;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
  .diagram__cap {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    text-align: center;
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .m-card {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 10%, var(--surface));
    stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 45%, var(--border));
    stroke-width: 1.5;
  }
  /* Memory differs: Claude writes it, not you - dashed edge marks the distinction. */
  .m-card--auto {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--objective) 12%, var(--surface));
    stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--objective) 55%, var(--border));
    stroke-dasharray: 5 4;
  }
  .m-name {
    fill: var(--text);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 650;
    text-anchor: middle;
  }
  .m-who {
    fill: var(--muted);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 11px;
    text-anchor: middle;
  }
  .m-feed {
    stroke: var(--border);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }
  .m-arrowhead {
    fill: var(--muted);
  }
  .m-chat {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 16%, var(--surface));
    stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 50%, var(--border));
    stroke-width: 1.5;
  }
  .m-chatname {
    fill: var(--text);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 13px;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-anchor: middle;
  }
</style>
